WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.982
The next set of solutions

00:00:00.982 --> 00:00:02.520
really deal with improving

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.920
the quality and timing of your sleep.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.360
Getting started right out the gates,

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.610
the most important thing you can do

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.290
to improve your sleep quality

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.640
is treating your sleep disorder

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.290
if you actually have one.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.480
There are a number of
really easy solutions

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.570
depending upon what your
sleep problem actually is.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.720
Think about this from the
perspective of a football player,

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:23.100
they're probably more likely

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.110
to be dealing with things like sleep apnea

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.086
because they're larger,

00:00:26.086 --> 00:00:28.170
relative to say maybe a swimmer

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:29.490
whose issue is more likely

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:30.960
to be dealing with circadian rhythms

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.180
because they're oftentimes practicing

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.130
and training early in the morning.

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:35.963
So right out the gates,

00:00:35.963 --> 00:00:38.820
just thinking about what
we're asking our bodies to do

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.470
gives us some strategies

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.769
for what type of disorder
we may likely have,

00:00:42.769 --> 00:00:45.840
and then of course then
the solution for that.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.340
In order to make my solutions

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.630
a little bit more straightforward,

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.750
what I want to do is talk
about getting to sleep faster

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.790
and then staying asleep more effectively.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:54.810
So what are the reasons

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:56.400
why people struggle to fall asleep

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.900
and how can we improve that?

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.820
It can be a number of different things,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.370
the obvious ones, but still important:

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.220
are we using stimulants
or caffeine too late

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:06.210
for our own physiology?

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.180
Some folks can metabolize
caffeine very quickly

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.013
and it doesn't affect them,

00:01:10.013 --> 00:01:12.180
others really struggle if it's within,

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.370
you know, eight or more hours of sleep.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.500
It can affect their overall sleep quality.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.460
That's a common one.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.550
Working or doing any
other stimulating events

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.870
at night can be a problem.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:23.340
Where people really miss this

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:27.600
is the best way to probably
think about it is novelty.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.610
So it's not the fact
that you're on your phone

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.560
or the fact that you're
watching TV or anything,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.150
it's seeking of novelty.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.220
That seems to be the big problem.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:36.930
So it's okay for some folks,

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.130
I will be candid with you,

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:39.810
I watch a little bit of TV

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.550
basically every night before sleep.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:42.900
But what you don't want to do are things

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.970
that cause you to seek novelty

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.130
and so your phone becomes problematic

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.350
because something new will pop up

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.090
and it'll reward that pattern

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.670
of seeing something new and
novel and get you excited.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:54.750
And so what you don't want to do

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.000
is watch TV shows that are that,

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:57.833
you don't want to watch things

00:01:57.833 --> 00:02:00.120
that are aggravating,
or arousing or exciting.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.260
You want to watch, typically,

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.000
things that are more interesting

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.247
but not overly stimulating.

00:02:05.247 --> 00:02:07.440
This is "Planet Earth."

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.020
This is not murder mysteries,

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.140
this is not things that get you aggravated

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.460
and irritated like the news

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.130
and political events and things like that.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:20.543
And so that is what you
want to be cautious of.

00:02:20.543 --> 00:02:23.700
Eating too close to bed can be a problem.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:27.420
However, however, and this is
important, people miss this.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:28.500
There's excellent research

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.150
showing small snacks before bed

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.920
can enhance sleep quality

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.180
and reduce sleep latency.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.970
Specifically, kiwis are well studied

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.040
as effective sleep aids.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.850
Higher glycemic index
meals pretty close to bed,

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.890
again, low amounts can be great.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.750
We certainly don't want
to eat high amounts

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.310
and increased digestion or heart rate

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.540
and problems like that,

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:49.830
but small snacks for some people

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:51.720
can actually be really effective

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.040
at putting you to sleep.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.440
Probably most important
to all this, though,

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.730
is really the pattern

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.280
and if you look around at
any of the sleep research

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.660
or talk to sleep experts,

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.010
they're going to talk about the need

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.020
to make sure that your
sleeping environment

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.850
is really a sleep sanctuary.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.400
Our body has probably one tool

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.270
better than anything else,

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.700
and that's the ability
to pattern recognize

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.179
and this becomes a problem.

00:03:13.179 --> 00:03:15.930
If you go into sleep environments

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.750
and you are anticipating
a pattern of sleep issues,

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.130
then that pattern is going to persist.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:21.600
If you have the opposite,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:22.947
then that pattern will persist.

00:03:22.947 --> 00:03:24.660
And so what we see a lot of times,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:25.710
our solution strategies

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.720
for people having a
hard time going to sleep

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:28.920
despite the fact that they're doing

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.300
all these other things right,

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:31.440
and this is common, right?

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:32.760
Oh, I don't have caffeine.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:35.550
I stop working, you
know, many hours before.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:38.250
I don't eat before, I meditate before,

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.720
I do breath work, I do all these things

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.820
and then I still struggle with sleep.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:42.653
Okay, great.

00:03:42.653 --> 00:03:44.550
I'm not watching TV,
all those other things.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.320
Why am I still struggling to fall asleep?

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.930
Well, oftentimes it's the fact

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.720
that you think about
struggling to fall asleep

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.730
that's causing that pattern to persist.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:54.930
So how do we solve that?

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.670
We take clocks away.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:57.660
You don't get to measure

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.100
and you don't get to see your sleep score.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.720
You don't get to see your
sleep latency number,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:01.740
you don't get to look at the clock

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.260
and think about how long it's
taking you to fall asleep.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.000
So all of those are also tools

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.950
to take that out of the equation

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:09.667
and reframe the approach into thinking,

00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:11.460
"When I get into bed,

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:13.800
this is an awesome opportunity to sleep,"

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.750
rather than, "Oh my gosh,
I'm going to get into bed

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.330
and have another night
where I can't fall asleep."

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:19.650
That's a bad framework to be in

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:20.973
and you want to get out of that

00:04:20.973 --> 00:04:23.100
as quickly as you possibly can.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.900
These environmental
pattern recognition things

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.270
are so strong, you can actually
use it to your advantage;

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.010
especially when traveling.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:30.900
Everyone knows of the first night effect,

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.730
which is when you are
in a new destination,

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.560
you struggle to sleep the first night

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.033
but tend to sleep a lot better the second

00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:37.920
or third and subsequent nights.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:39.750
Well a lot of that is because

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.400
you're in a different environment, right?

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.320
We aren't seeing and smelling and hearing

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:44.730
the same things we did in our house.

00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:46.980
So if we can artificially
create that in our house

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.450
and take that on the road,

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.940
we can dramatically
improve our sleep quality

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.790
and specifically our sleep onset.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.130
Think about smell as the easiest one.

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:57.711
If you take a scent that you like,

00:04:57.711 --> 00:04:59.379
common ones are lavender,

00:04:59.379 --> 00:05:00.780
and you put a little lavender

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.760
on your bed at night in
your house every night,

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.700
you stand to associate
that smell with safe,

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.340
secure, high quality sleep, et cetera.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.260
You take that on the road with you, then,

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.910
put that on your new mattress

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.260
or bed or wherever you go.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.000
Those same sense will kick in,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:16.530
the same pattern recognition will happen,

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:19.860
and ideally you'll have
the same quality of sleep.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.080
You can do a similar thing
with temperature, right?

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.540
If you have a set temperature of your bed,

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:25.590
you may not be able to take

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.990
that entire cooling mattress
with you on the road,

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.240
but there are things like cooling pillows.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.730
So you use that same temperature
on your pillow at home,

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:34.800
take that pillow or pillowcase with you,

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.690
and now you have a mimicked environment.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:38.610
You can combine a couple of
different things like that

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.040
and you should really
improve your sleep latency

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.150
when you're on the road.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:43.650
Now when it comes to
staying asleep at night,

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:45.690
there's a bunch of different
solutions we can talk about.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:49.080
One of my most common ones is hydration.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:50.310
If you drink too much water,

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.770
of course you're going to
wake up and have to urinate

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:54.512
and that's going to cause
you to get up a whole bunch.

00:05:54.512 --> 00:05:56.920
But really, some folks don't understand

00:05:57.828 --> 00:06:00.750
that sleep quality will
be a big determinant

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:03.330
upon how frequently you get up to go pee.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:06.390
If you are waking up more
than once and you go to pee,

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:09.090
there's a large volume there, it is clear

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.010
and that's happening
the next morning as well

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.930
when you wake up and you
still have a lot of pee,

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.930
then you probably have an issue

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:17.580
of drinking too much pure water.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.290
In that case, just
reduce your water intake

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:20.923
for a couple of hours prior to bed

00:06:20.923 --> 00:06:22.759
and you should be fine.

00:06:22.759 --> 00:06:24.952
Funny enough, we've had plenty

00:06:24.952 --> 00:06:28.230
of our high performing clients,

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:30.840
our high performing executives
in our coaching programs

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.760
come to us with all kinds of problems

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.850
with their physiology and
performance and brain fog,

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:38.220
and really all of it
boiled down to the fact

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:40.320
that they drank way too
much water at night.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:41.970
It was tanking their sleep.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.817
We moved that and within days,

00:06:43.817 --> 00:06:45.420
they're sleeping for ten hours

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:47.050
for the first time in 15 years.

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:49.389
I have so many examples of that

00:06:49.389 --> 00:06:51.120
where it's pretty silly, honestly,

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.190
where I'm like man, you
spent a lot of money and time

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.500
to figure out you drink way
too much water at night.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.750
So, it can be quite that simple.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.400
Other times, though, it's
a little bit deceiving;

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.370
so folks will oftentimes
think that's the problem.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:03.330
If it's not the case,

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:05.670
you're waking up and there's
not a lot of volume there

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:06.503
or there is like one time

00:07:06.503 --> 00:07:09.600
and then the second time it's a lot less

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.850
or it's you know, more yellow colored,

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:12.810
or in the morning you wake up

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:14.280
and realize you didn't even pee

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.710
for 45 minutes after waking up,

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:19.140
this is sometimes an
issue of sleep quality.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:21.990
And so what happens is typically at night,

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:23.790
you're supposed to be sending
signals to your kidneys

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.580
that says hey, stop filtering
and producing urine.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:29.310
And so obviously we don't want
to be waking up to do that

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:30.510
all throughout the night.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:32.670
If you're of a low quality sleep though,

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:33.750
for any number of reasons,

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:35.550
that signal can continue to be sent

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:37.290
and so you can continue to make the urine.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:39.090
And so people think
that their sleep quality

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:40.530
is being ruined by the fact

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:41.850
that they have to get up and pee,

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:43.590
when the honest reality is they're peeing

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:45.660
because they're already basically awake.

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:47.640
And so that is another way to figure out,

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.200
hmm, maybe I have a disorder,

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.340
maybe I should get checked out,

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:51.720
maybe something is going on

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:52.980
'cause I'm waking up to pee a lot

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.540
and really there's not a lot there;

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.190
I probably should get checked out.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.530
Another really common
one is sleep position.

00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:01.110
We see this routinely where

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:03.270
80 plus percentages of
people's sleep problems

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:05.610
happen when they're on one
position of their body.

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:06.841
Most commonly it's your back.

00:08:06.841 --> 00:08:09.390
And so avoiding sleeping
directly on your back

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:10.482
and sleeping either on your sides,

00:08:10.482 --> 00:08:13.392
as a blanket rule though
not true for everyone,

00:08:13.392 --> 00:08:15.720
can really dramatically
improve people's sleep.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:18.300
I've told this story earlier

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.980
of one of my professional athletes

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.390
I've worked with having this issue,

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:23.610
but we have seen this time and time again.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.280
And so getting some assessment or analysis

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:28.650
of what's happening in
your sleep position,

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:30.420
this is a similar story to ergonomics.

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:32.340
So making sure that your bed and mattress

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.350
and pillow are all comfortable

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:37.020
can also be really important factors

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:38.760
to your overall sleep quality.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.290
Outside of that, we start now talking

00:08:40.290 --> 00:08:41.730
about environmental factors.

00:08:41.730 --> 00:08:43.440
You probably have heard ad nauseam

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:45.780
about of course temperature.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:47.490
You want to be comfortable there

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:48.900
and we want to be probably
a little bit cooler

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.190
than we are during the day,

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:51.540
but even noise.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.700
We want to be less than 35 decibels.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.680
Anything above that
tends to create problems.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.230
This is where actually
things like pink noise

00:08:58.230 --> 00:08:59.063
start to come into play.

00:08:59.063 --> 00:09:00.120
There's a lot of really interesting

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:01.920
research coming out on that.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.810
It's a little bit deeper than white noise

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:06.930
and it's not really publicly
available for the most part,

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:09.870
but the research thus
far is quite impressive.

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:13.290
The issue with white
noise or sleep machines

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.780
is the fact that above 35 decibels,

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:17.430
they can actually create sleep problems

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:18.870
because they're so loud.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:19.980
And so while it's great

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:22.560
to kind of drown out the
background information,

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:23.610
you don't want to be in a position

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:25.260
where you're actually too loud.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:26.520
Depending on where you live,

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.260
if you're especially
in an urban environment

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:31.860
and you have lots of external
sound stuff going on,

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.060
you may want to actually turn to

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:34.710
something like earplugs

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:37.380
instead of a sleep
machine for that purpose.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:39.720
Though, earplugs may create problems

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:42.060
with ear pain and discomfort
and stuff like that.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:44.520
So again, choose the best tool

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:46.230
that you have for your situation.

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.990
But really, below 35 decibels

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:50.760
is the gold standard there.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:52.080
Again, ideally in the show notes here,

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:53.640
we've got the link to my paper

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.306
that you can go through all 11 factors,

00:09:55.306 --> 00:09:58.320
but other ones that are
really common are CO2.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:09:59.780
There's emerging evidence

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:02.760
that when that thing gets
above 900 parts per million

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.400
and then really specifically
in exaggerated scenarios

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:08.610
of above like 2,400 parts per million,

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:12.000
that this can dramatically
impact sleep quality,

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.580
sleep onset and sleep latency;

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:16.410
as well as next day wakefulness,

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:17.970
memory and decision making,

00:10:17.970 --> 00:10:21.270
and a bunch of other
cognitive performance tasks.

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:23.460
So many, many other factors

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:25.898
that go into your sleep quality

00:10:25.898 --> 00:10:27.313
such as your environment,

00:10:27.313 --> 00:10:29.190
that will have in the show notes.

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:31.710
Last thing I want to talk
about here is snoring.

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:34.890
Now I don't want to overly concern you,

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:37.860
everybody snores at some point or another.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:39.120
It doesn't necessarily mean,

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:40.829
it probably doesn't mean
you have a sleep disorder,

00:10:40.829 --> 00:10:42.510
though it's not totally benign

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:44.340
if you're a chronic or excessive snorer;

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:45.960
that's really the problem here.

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:47.880
It's not a normal function.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:50.370
It's common, but it's not normal.

00:10:50.370 --> 00:10:52.410
Now one thing that's actually quite funny

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:55.195
that we laugh about on my team a lot,

00:10:55.195 --> 00:10:58.830
is how frequently people
come into our programs

00:10:58.830 --> 00:10:59.880
because of sleep issues,

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:01.380
and they've tried everything.

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.450
They've done all the
stuff we've talked about,

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.610
they've had sleep studies done before,

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:08.400
and they came back either
like really moderate

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:09.870
or nothing at all

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:12.180
and they maybe think
their testosterone is low

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:13.050
or maybe it is;

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:14.280
and they've run through everything,

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:16.170
like I just cannot sleep.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:18.480
And we do one little test on him

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:22.740
and realize, wow, has your
sleep partner been assessed?

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:25.980
Like, oh my sleep partner
just snores like crazy.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:27.360
And we always chuckle 'cause I'm like man,

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:28.590
I got really bad news for you.

00:11:28.590 --> 00:11:30.390
Your sleep is fine,

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:32.953
but your partner's sleep
is a complete disaster

00:11:32.953 --> 00:11:34.590
and they're ruining your sleep.

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:36.720
So we've had a lot of
people come into our program

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.310
and then basically switch.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:39.750
So the person that paid initially said,

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:40.680
don't even do me anymore,

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:41.750
do this on my sleep partner,

00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:43.440
on my husband or my wife, whatever.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:44.430
Fix their sleep.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:46.290
And then that original
person's sleep disorder

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:49.320
quote-unquote goes away
immediately and permanently.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:50.190
And this has happened,

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:51.390
like you can hear me literally laughing,

00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:52.950
'cause it's happened so many times

00:11:52.950 --> 00:11:53.783
and I don't feel bad about it

00:11:53.783 --> 00:11:55.680
'cause the people sleep
perfectly afterwards

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:56.820
because we found the real problem

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:58.680
and it had nothing to do with them.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:01.440
And so snoring can be a
real problem with sleep.

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:04.440
And so really making
sure that it's not you,

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:05.490
it's not your sleeping partner,

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:08.490
or a lot of the cases it's their dog.

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:10.140
Not often cats are snoring that loud,

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:11.730
but dogs that's really, really common.

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:13.770
And so that that can be really disruptive

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:15.030
to overall sleep.

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:16.230
If it is you personally,

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:17.940
we'll go back to that example.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:21.240
You can try just going with
something like mouth tape.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.457
I've done that a ton, I use it a ton,

00:12:23.457 --> 00:12:26.280
especially using a
combination of nasal dilators;

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:28.380
so either nasal strips
or something like that

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:31.560
to open up your nasal passageways
as well as mouth tape.

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:33.210
And I will be candid with you,

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:34.620
we've had a lot of success with that.

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.060
And sometimes that step alone

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:37.920
really can improve snoring

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:40.350
and solve a lot of sleep problems.

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:41.700
It doesn't always happen, though.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:43.170
So if it doesn't, then
you probably need to go

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:44.730
to the next step one way or the other.

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:46.860
But it can absolutely be a viable solution

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:48.420
and is a nice place to start.

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:49.500
And just to be really clear,

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:51.000
the next step I'm talking about

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:53.128
is usually getting a sleep study done,

00:12:53.128 --> 00:12:54.690
but you can try other stuff as well.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:55.530
We've already talked about,

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:58.050
position is another common solution.

00:12:58.050 --> 00:12:59.880
Generally people don't
snore on their sides

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:02.250
and if they do, not
nearly as much or as loud

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:03.780
as they are when they're on their back.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:06.210
So just try investing in
one of those sleep pillows

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:07.500
I talked about earlier

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:09.720
that forces you to sleep
on your left or right side.

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:12.081
Sometimes that solves
the problem immediately.

00:13:12.081 --> 00:13:14.280
There are also tongue
exercises you can do.

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:15.330
We can actually have

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:17.134
a whole protocol here in the show notes.

00:13:17.134 --> 00:13:20.130
This is very free, it's
going to take typically

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:23.010
four to eight weeks to have
some sort of an effect,

00:13:23.010 --> 00:13:24.240
but there is strong research

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:26.460
behind tongue strengthening exercises

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:28.230
to resolve that problem.

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:29.700
Sometimes there is no solution,

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:32.400
it is anatomical and
you either have to have

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:34.380
some sort of nasal surgery

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:38.220
or put in some sort of a
mouthpiece to resolve that problem,

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.740
and then sometimes not even that works.

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:41.910
But for the most part,

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:45.000
something in that solution
that I just went over

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:46.710
is going to either dramatically help,

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:49.290
if not completely solve
your snoring problems.

00:13:49.290 --> 00:13:50.430
If you enjoyed this clip,

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:53.292
you can watch the full
episode by clicking here.

00:13:53.292 --> 00:13:56.042
[music]

